{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788353051,"nonce":"FNxDrt7Iv1vGT8oOQT4cw4ZyUgk=","message":"`RimWorld/WorkGiver_DoBill.cs StartOrResumeBillJob`, the only write:\n\n```csharp\nbill.nextTickToSearchForIngredients =\n    Find.TickManager.TicksGame + ReCheckFailedBillTicksRange.RandomInRange;\n```\n\nand, in the same method, the only read:\n\n```csharp\nFind.TickManager.TicksGame \u003c= bill.nextTickToSearchForIngredients\n    \u0026\u0026 FloatMenuMakerMap.makingFor != pawn\n```\n\nwith `private static readonly IntRange ReCheckFailedBillTicksRange = new IntRange(500, 600);`\n\n**The back-off is 500–600 ticks — ten game seconds — and it REARMS on every\nfailure.** So the field cannot \"stay dead long after they are unforbidden\" and\ncannot \"sit in the future for days\"; the post-mortem's `3606060` was about a\nthousand ticks ahead of `now`, not days. This issue and\n`RUNS/m1-20260901/postmortem.md` both say otherwise and both are wrong on that\npoint.\n\n**The real problem is worse than the one described.** Because it rearms, a\nstarving bill's field is in the future essentially always, and **one sample\ncannot distinguish one failed search from ten thousand.** Publishing the raw\ntick and leaving the caller to compare it with `now` is therefore not merely\ninconvenient — the question is unanswerable from one read, while the number\ninvites the reader to think it has been answered. That is why nobody looked at\nit: looking would not have helped.\n\nSecond thing worth recording: `FloatMenuMakerMap.makingFor != pawn` means a\n`prioritize`-driven probe does NOT write the field. So `prioritize`'s `blocked:`\nreason and this counter are genuinely independent readings, which is why the run\ncould corner the defect at all.\n\n\n**Item 1 — a state, not a tick.** `bills` publishes `ingredient_search`:\n\n```\nstate                        \"asleep\" | \"ready\" | \"unknown\"\nwakes_tick                   the raw stamp\nwakes_in_ticks               max(0, stamp - now)\nconsecutive_failed_searches  see below\nasleep_since_tick            when the current streak began\nasleep_for_ticks             now - asleep_since_tick, when a streak is observed\nobserved                     whether the count is backed by observation\nobserved_since_tick          when the watch armed\nnote                         the mechanism, in one paragraph\n```\n\nThe raw `next_ingredient_search_tick` stays published beside it, because\n`32b9e01` uses it as proof and that use is unaffected.\n\n**Item 2 — the count.** The game stores a timestamp, not a counter, and a\nSUCCESSFUL search leaves no trace at all, so the count is derived.\n`BillWatch.Tick` samples every **250 ticks** — deliberately under the 500-tick\nminimum back-off, so no distinct failure can slip between two samples — over\n`ListerThings.ThingsInGroup(PotentialBillGiver)`, and counts stamp CHANGES,\nsince the only writer of that field is the failed-search branch. A streak clears\nonce the stamp has been stale for 2,500 ticks (~4x the maximum back-off); it is\nNOT cleared merely because `now \u003e stamp`, which is true for most of every window\nand would reset the counter continuously.\n\nThe number is a **floor observed since `observed_since_tick`**, and the block\nsays so in the same breath rather than pretending to know the colony's whole\nhistory. First sight of a bill counts nothing — a fresh `bill-add` has stamp 0,\nand a sentinel would have reported every new bill as already failing.\n\nCost: one int read per bill per 250 ticks; the method returns on its first line\n249 ticks out of 250. It sits beside `ColonySampler.Tick` in\n`AgentGameComponent.GameComponentTick` for the same reason that one does — a\nper-FRAME observer would count wall clock while the agent sat thinking.\n\n**Item 3 — the distinction, and it is the load-bearing one.** `health` on every\nbill line is one word, and the two that matter are different words:\n\n- `asleep-will-retry` — backed off, and there IS a usable ingredient on the map.\n  It will pick up on its own. Nothing to do.\n- `asleep-no-matching-ingredient` — backed off, and NOTHING on the map can\n  satisfy it. This is the m1-20260901 state, and `remedy` names the verb that\n  changes it — or says that no verb does, which for `eef837a`'s actual defect\n  (a rotting corpse against `ButcherCorpseFlesh.fixedIngredientFilter`'s\n  `AllowRotten` exclusion) is the true answer.\n\nThe difference is computed from `ingredient_match`, which runs\n`WorkGiver_DoBill`'s own ingredient predicate over the things on the map — see\nthe `eef837a` comment. `filter-empty`, `no-ingredient-filter`, `suspended` and\n`research-missing` are in the same ladder, so a bill for an unresearched recipe\ncan no longer report `workable`.\n\n**Item 4 — `checklists/turn.md`.** New trip-wire `bill-produces-nothing`, at the\nTURN level rather than the daily one, because that is where the loop already\nreads. It cites the field, says why the raw tick is not the read, and names the\n`remedy` field as the act. `checklists/daily.md`'s `production-still-runs` is\nrewritten off `health` and its `retire-when` now says honestly which half\nshipped (creation-time diagnosis) and which half has not (worker eligibility).\n\n**Item 5 — covered by `eef837a`'s script**, as this issue asked. Phase 5 of\n`accept/eef837a-bill-filter.py` starves a bill, runs the clock past the back-off\nso a REAL failed search fires, asserts the whole block, then restores the\ningredient and asserts the verdict CHANGED — an inequality between two staged\nstates, which is the only form of that assertion a constant cannot satisfy.\nPhase 5 needs a bench and has not been run; the constants it depends on\n(`ReCheckFailedBillTicksRange`, `BillWatch.Cadence \u003c 500`) are re-derived\noffline by `--selftest`, which is 28/28.","files":null}]}